/**
\fn Port pdg::GraphicsManager::createOffscreenPort(Rect rect)
\brief Create a transparent drawing surface without opening a window.

The returned Port supports the usual drawing methods, fonts, Attributes, retained
Drawings, and clipping. It starts with transparent black pixels. Drawing persists
between calls; the frame loop does not clear the surface or send it PortDraw events.
Draw directly whenever its contents need updating.

\param rect Its width and height must be positive whole numbers within the graphics
backend's texture-size limit. Its position is ignored; the drawing area starts at (0, 0).
\return A Port managed by GraphicsManager, or null if the size is invalid or the
backend cannot allocate the surface. Release it with closeGraphicsPort(). Do not
use a Port after closing it.

\note Requires a graphics-enabled build, including web builds. A main port or visible
window is not required. Offscreen ports do not become the main port. If a main port
exists at creation, they share its graphics context; closing that window also closes
its offscreen ports. Images already created from them remain valid.
\note API stability: 2 — Evolving.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const surface = pdg.gfx.createOffscreenPort(new pdg.Rect(256, 128));
if (!surface) throw new Error('Could not allocate drawing surface');
surface.drawRect(new pdg.Rect(16, 16, 240, 112),
    new pdg.Attributes().fillColor('blue').lineStyle(pdg.lineStyle_None));
const image = new pdg.Image(surface, pdg.CopyPixels);
pdg.gfx.closeGraphicsPort(surface);
// image can now be used with Port.drawImage(), Attributes.texture(), or a Sprite.
\endcode
\see Image::Image(Port, boolean), closeGraphicsPort()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Image::Image(Port port, boolean copyPixels)
\brief Create an independent snapshot or a live image of an offscreen surface.

\param port An open Port returned by GraphicsManager::createOffscreenPort().
\param copyPixels Defaults to pdg.CopyPixels (true): copy the current RGBA pixels into
an independent image. With pdg.SharedSurface (false), retain the shared drawing surface:
subsequent Port drawing is visible the next time the image is drawn, without constructing
another image or uploading pixels when both use the same graphics context. Literal
booleans remain supported; an omitted or undefined flag uses CopyPixels.

Creates an Image with the port's dimensions and transparency. Throws an Error for an
onscreen port and TypeError for a non-boolean copyPixels. Use new with this constructor.

Both kinds of image remain usable after the source port closes. Closing a port with
live images prevents further drawing through that port but retains its final surface
until the last live image is released. Closing the underlying graphics context preserves
its final pixels for surviving images. Drawing does not depend on which clip rectangle
was active when the image was created: the image always represents the whole surface.

Pixel reads and serialization of live images read the current contents. Serialized
images are snapshots and do not restore a connection to a Port. Drawing across independent
graphics contexts, or drawing a live image back into its own source, uses a pixel snapshot.
Calling setTransparentColor() on a live image converts that image to an independent
snapshot before applying the color key.

\note Requires a graphics-enabled build. Shared images retain graphics memory until
released, even after closeGraphicsPort(). An image's opacity is independent of the port.
\note API stability: 2 — Evolving.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const surface = pdg.gfx.createOffscreenPort(new pdg.Rect(128, 128));
if (!surface) throw new Error('Could not allocate drawing surface');
const live = new pdg.Image(surface, pdg.SharedSurface);
surface.drawEllipse(new pdg.Point(64, 64), 40, 40,
    new pdg.Attributes().fillColor('red').lineStyle(pdg.lineStyle_None));
// Drawing live now shows the red circle; further surface drawing updates it too.
const frozen = new pdg.Image(surface, pdg.CopyPixels);
// frozen keeps this circle even when the surface changes.
\endcode
\see GraphicsManager::createOffscreenPort(), Port::drawImage(), getPixel()
*/

/**
\var pdg::CopyPixels
\brief Copy an offscreen surface's current pixels into an independent Image.
Read-only boolean constant equal to true. The default for Image::Image(Port, boolean).
Available in every JavaScript runtime;
constructing images from a Port requires graphics support.
\ingroup Graphics
*/

/**
\var pdg::SharedSurface
\brief Share an offscreen surface so later Port drawing updates the Image.
Read-only boolean constant equal to false. Use new pdg.Image(port, pdg.SharedSurface).
The Image retains the surface after the source Port closes. Available in every
JavaScript runtime; constructing images from a Port requires graphics support.
\ingroup Graphics
*/

/**
\fn undefined pdg::Port::clear(Color color)
\brief Replace pixels inside the current clip with an exact color.
\param color Defaults to transparent black. Alpha replaces the previous alpha;
this differs from drawing a translucent rectangle, which blends with existing pixels.

Works on graphics-enabled onscreen and offscreen ports. The current clip is unchanged.
Live images made from an offscreen port reflect the clear on their next draw.
\note API stability: 2 — Evolving.
\see setClipRect(), resetClipRect(), GraphicsManager::createOffscreenPort()
*/

/**
\fn undefined pdg::Port::setDrawingOrigin(Point origin)
\brief Set the Port coordinate at the top left of an offscreen surface.
\param origin Finite coordinates for the drawing area's top left. Initially (0, 0).

Pixel dimensions and stored pixels do not change. getDrawingArea() reports the new
origin and the clip resets to that area. Drawing and clipping now use those coordinates;
Image pixel coordinates remain zero-based. This supports rendering a View into a reusable
surface without changing its layout coordinates. Onscreen ports and nonfinite origins
are rejected with an error.
\note Requires a Port from GraphicsManager::createOffscreenPort().
\note API stability: 2 — Evolving.
\see clear(), getDrawingArea(), Image::Image(Port, boolean)
*/
